  <div class="legend">
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#1f4a8c"></span>raw sample dimension $n$</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#2d7a3e"></span>dimension of minimal sufficient $T$</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#d4690a"></span>no finite reduction</span>
  </div>
</Fragment>
<Fragment slot="controls">
  <ControlRange id="comp-n" label="sample size $n$" valueId="comp-n-v" min="2" max="200" step="1" value="40" />
</Fragment>
</Figure>

<script is:inline>
(() => {
  const canvas = document.getElementById("fig-compress");
  const nIn = document.getElementById("comp-n");
  const nV = document.getElementById("comp-n-v");
  const readout = document.getElementById("compress-readout");

  const FAMS = [
    { name: "Bernoulli(p)", T: "Σxᵢ", dim: 1, ok: true },
    { name: "Poisson(λ)", T: "Σxᵢ", dim: 1, ok: true },
    { name: "Exponential(λ)", T: "Σxᵢ", dim: 1, ok: true },
    { name: "Normal(μ, 1)", T: "x̄", dim: 1, ok: true },
    { name: "Uniform(0, θ)", T: "max(xᵢ)", dim: 1, ok: true },
    { name: "Normal(μ, σ²)", T: "(Σxᵢ, Σxᵢ²)", dim: 2, ok: true },
    { name: "Gamma(α, β)", T: "(Σxᵢ, Σlog xᵢ)", dim: 2, ok: true },
    { name: "Cauchy(θ, 1)", T: "order statistic", dim: null, ok: false },
    { name: "Mixture of 2 Gaussians", T: "order statistic", dim: null, ok: false },
  ];

  function setup(c) {
    const ratio = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
    const iw = +c.getAttribute("width"), ih = +c.getAttribute("height");
    c.style.width = "100%"; c.style.height = `${c.getBoundingClientRect().width * ih / iw}px`;
    const r = c.getBoundingClientRect();
    c.width = r.width * ratio; c.height = r.height * ratio;
    const ctx = c.getContext("2d"); ctx.scale(ratio, ratio);
    return { ctx, w: r.width, h: r.height };
  }

  function draw() {
    const { ctx, w, h } = setup(canvas);
    const n = +nIn.value;
    nV.textContent = n;

    ctx.fillStyle = "#fff"; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);
    const padL = 160, padR = 32, padT = 22, padB = 28;
    const plotW = w - padL - padR, plotH = h - padT - padB;
    const rowH = plotH / FAMS.length;
    const xMax = Math.max(n, 20);
    const xS = (x) => padL + (x / xMax) * plotW;

    FAMS.forEach((f, i) => {
      const y0 = padT + i * rowH + rowH * 0.18;
      const bh = rowH * 0.34;
      // label
      ctx.fillStyle = "#1f2733"; ctx.font = "11px -apple-system, sans-serif"; ctx.textAlign = "right";
      ctx.fillText(f.name, padL - 8, y0 + bh + 4);
      ctx.fillStyle = "#94a3b8"; ctx.font = "10px 'SF Mono', monospace";
      ctx.fillText(`T = ${f.T}`, padL - 8, y0 + bh + 17);

      // raw bar
      ctx.fillStyle = "#1f4a8c"; ctx.fillRect(padL, y0, xS(n) - padL, bh);
      ctx.fillStyle = "#fff"; ctx.font = "10px -apple-system, sans-serif"; ctx.textAlign = "right";
      ctx.fillText(`n = ${n}`, xS(n) - 4, y0 + bh - 3);

      // T bar
      const ty = y0 + bh + 3;
      if (f.ok) {
        ctx.fillStyle = "#2d7a3e"; ctx.fillRect(padL, ty, xS(f.dim) - padL, bh);
        ctx.fillStyle = "#fff"; ctx.textAlign = "left";
        ctx.fillText(`dim = ${f.dim}`, padL + 4, ty + bh - 3);
      } else {
        ctx.fillStyle = "#d4690a"; ctx.fillRect(padL, ty, xS(n) - padL, bh);
        ctx.fillStyle = "#fff"; ctx.textAlign = "left";
        ctx.fillText(`dim = n; no compression`, padL + 4, ty + bh - 3);
      }
    });

    // x axis ticks at top of last row + below
    ctx.fillStyle = "#8892a3"; ctx.font = "10px -apple-system, sans-serif"; ctx.textAlign = "center";
    for (let k = 0; k <= 4; k++) {
      const v = Math.round(k / 4 * xMax);
      ctx.fillText(v.toString(), xS(v), padT + plotH + 14);
    }

    readout.innerHTML =
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">most exponential-family models compress</span><span>n samples → 1 or 2 numbers</span></div>` +
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">Cauchy and Gaussian mixtures</span><span>n samples → n numbers (no reduction)</span></div>` +
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">Pitman–Koopman–Darmois</span><span>finite-dim sufficient T ⇔ exponential family</span></div>`;
  }

  nIn.addEventListener("input", draw);
  window.addEventListener("resize", draw);
  draw();
})();
</script>

<Aside>
  <strong>Pitman–Koopman–Darmois.</strong> Under regularity conditions (the
  support doesn't depend on $\theta$, and the family is suitably smooth), a
  finite-dimensional sufficient statistic exists for all $n$ <em>only</em> for
  exponential families. So the Cauchy row above is a structural impossibility,
  not an oversight. The Uniform$(0,\theta)$ row is the opposite kind
  of edge case: it does compress to $\max(x_i)$, but the support depends on
  $\theta$, which violates the theorem's regularity. PKD applies cleanly only
  to the exponential-family rows. §7 makes the converse direction visible.
</Aside>

<h2>6. Rao–Blackwell: variance collapse</h2>

<p>
Rao–Blackwell says: take any unbiased estimator $\hat\theta(X)$, replace it
with its conditional expectation given a sufficient statistic, and the result
is unbiased and has lower (or equal) variance. The variance decomposition
$$\operatorname&#123;Var&#125;(\hat\theta) \;=\; \mathbb&#123;E&#125;[\operatorname&#123;Var&#125;(\hat\theta \mid T)] + \operatorname&#123;Var&#125;(\mathbb&#123;E&#125;[\hat\theta \mid T])$$
splits the variance into what conditioning on $T$ discards and what it keeps.
The first piece is gone for free.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 6 · Bad estimator → Rao–Blackwellized estimator" canvasId="fig-rao" width="760" height="360" readoutId="rao-readout">
<Fragment slot="legend">
  <div class="legend">
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:rgba(31,74,140,0.55)"></span>samples of $\hat p = x_1$</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:rgba(184,65,42,0.7)"></span>samples of $\mathbb&#123;E&#125;[x_1 \mid \sum x_i] = \bar x$</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:#0f172a"></span>true $p$</span>
  </div>
</Fragment>
<Fragment slot="controls">
  <ControlRange id="rao-p" label="true $p$" valueId="rao-p-v" min="0.05" max="0.95" step="0.01" value="0.4" />
  <ControlRange id="rao-n" label="sample size $n$" valueId="rao-n-v" min="2" max="80" step="1" value="20" />
  <PresetRow>
    <button id="rao-resample">Resample</button>
  </PresetRow>
</Fragment>
</Figure>

<script is:inline>
(() => {
  const canvas = document.getElementById("fig-rao");
  const pIn = document.getElementById("rao-p");
  const nIn = document.getElementById("rao-n");
  const pV = document.getElementById("rao-p-v");
  const nV = document.getElementById("rao-n-v");
  const readout = document.getElementById("rao-readout");
  const resampleBtn = document.getElementById("rao-resample");

  let seed = 41;
  function rng() { seed = (seed * 16807) % 2147483647; return seed / 2147483647; }
  const M = 2000;
  function trial(p, n) {
    const out = { bad: [], rb: [] };
    for (let i = 0; i < M; i++) {
      let s = 0, first = 0;
      for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) { const b = rng() < p ? 1 : 0; if (j === 0) first = b; s += b; }
      out.bad.push(first);
      out.rb.push(s / n);
    }
    return out;
  }
  let cur = null;
  function regen() { cur = trial(+pIn.value, +nIn.value); }

  function setup(c) {
    const ratio = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
    const iw = +c.getAttribute("width"), ih = +c.getAttribute("height");
    c.style.width = "100%"; c.style.height = `${c.getBoundingClientRect().width * ih / iw}px`;
    const r = c.getBoundingClientRect();
    c.width = r.width * ratio; c.height = r.height * ratio;
    const ctx = c.getContext("2d"); ctx.scale(ratio, ratio);
    return { ctx, w: r.width, h: r.height };
  }

  function draw() {
    if (!cur) regen();
    const { ctx, w, h } = setup(canvas);
    const p = +pIn.value, n = +nIn.value;
    pV.textContent = p.toFixed(2); nV.textContent = n;

    ctx.fillStyle = "#fff"; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);
    const padL = 60, padR = 24, padT = 30, padB = 60;
    const plotW = w - padL - padR, plotH = h - padT - padB;
    ctx.strokeStyle = "#e3ddd0"; ctx.strokeRect(padL, padT, plotW, plotH);

    const xMin = 0, xMax = 1;
    const xS = (x) => padL + (x - xMin) / (xMax - xMin) * plotW;

    // histograms
    const bins = 40;
    const hBad = new Array(bins).fill(0), hRB = new Array(bins).fill(0);
    cur.bad.forEach(v => { const k = Math.min(bins - 1, Math.floor(v * bins)); hBad[k]++; });
    cur.rb.forEach(v => { const k = Math.min(bins - 1, Math.floor(v * bins)); hRB[k]++; });
    const mx = Math.max(...hBad, ...hRB);
    const drawHist = (h0, color) => {
      ctx.fillStyle = color;
      for (let k = 0; k < bins; k++) {
        const x0 = xS(k / bins), x1 = xS((k + 1) / bins);
        const bh = (h0[k] / mx) * (plotH - 30);
        ctx.fillRect(x0, padT + plotH - bh, x1 - x0, bh);
      }
    };
    drawHist(hBad, "rgba(31,74,140,0.45)");
    drawHist(hRB, "rgba(184,65,42,0.65)");

    // true p marker
    ctx.strokeStyle = "#0f172a"; ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(xS(p), padT); ctx.lineTo(xS(p), padT + plotH); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.fillStyle = "#0f172a"; ctx.font = "11px -apple-system, sans-serif"; ctx.textAlign = "center";
    ctx.fillText(`true p = ${p.toFixed(2)}`, xS(p), padT - 6);

    // x ticks
    ctx.fillStyle = "#8892a3"; ctx.font = "10px -apple-system, sans-serif"; ctx.textAlign = "center";
    for (let k = 0; k <= 5; k++) {
      const v = k / 5;
      ctx.fillText(v.toFixed(1), xS(v), padT + plotH + 14);
    }
    ctx.fillStyle = "#475569"; ctx.font = "11px -apple-system, sans-serif";
    ctx.fillText("estimator value", padL + plotW / 2, padT + plotH + 30);

    // variance bars at bottom
    const meanBad = cur.bad.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / M;
    const meanRB = cur.rb.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / M;
    const varBad = cur.bad.reduce((a, b) => a + (b - meanBad) ** 2, 0) / M;
    const varRB = cur.rb.reduce((a, b) => a + (b - meanRB) ** 2, 0) / M;
    const ratio = varRB / Math.max(varBad, 1e-9);

    readout.innerHTML =
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">Var(x₁) (bad estimator)</span><span>${varBad.toFixed(4)}</span></div>` +
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">Var(x̄) = Var(E[x₁|T]) (Rao–Blackwellized)</span><span>${varRB.toFixed(4)}</span></div>` +
      `<div class="row"><span class="lbl">variance ratio</span><span>${ratio.toFixed(3)} ≈ 1/n = ${(1/n).toFixed(3)}</span></div>`;
  }

  [pIn, nIn].forEach(i => i.addEventListener("input", () => { regen(); draw(); }));
  resampleBtn.addEventListener("click", () => { regen(); draw(); });
  window.addEventListener("resize", draw);
  draw();
})();
</script>

<p>
Both estimators are unbiased for $p$; their histograms sit over the same true
value. But the bad estimator (which discards $n - 1$ samples) has variance
$p(1-p)$ regardless of $n$, while the Rao–Blackwellized one has variance
$p(1-p)/n$. The factor $n$ is the variance you got back by
conditioning on a sufficient statistic.
</p>

<h2>7. The exponential family: where sufficiency comes from</h2>

<p>
Every family in §5 with finite-dimensional $T$ is an <strong>exponential
family</strong>: it can be written as
</p>

$$
p(x \mid \eta) \;=\; h(x)\,\exp\!\Bigl[\eta^\top T(x) - A(\eta)\Bigr].
$$

<p>
The factorization theorem is built in — $T(x)$ is staring at you in the
exponent. <strong>Pitman–Koopman–Darmois</strong> is the converse: under
regularity (fixed support, smooth densities), this is the <em>only</em> way a
finite-dimensional sufficient statistic exists for every sample size $n$.
Conjugate priors with finite-dimensional hyperparameters are therefore
restricted to this case; outside it, the posterior's complexity grows with $n$.
</p>

<p>
The <a href="/math/exponential-family/">exponential family</a> page covers what
$\eta$, $T(x)$, $h(x)$, and $A(\eta)$ are called and why (the log-partition
borrows from statistical physics, $A$'s derivatives give the cumulants of
$T(X)$, the Fisher metric is its Hessian, the logit and log link in GLMs are
the canonical link for the Bernoulli and Poisson members), and includes an
interactive picker stepping through six standard members.
</p>

<h2>8. The flow diagram: one picture, three currencies</h2>

<OrientationNote>
  <p>
    The flow diagram recasts sufficiency through conditional independence,
    data processing, Fisher information, and mutual information instead of only
    through factorization.
  </p>
</OrientationNote>

<p>
The generative model is always a Markov chain $\theta \to X \to T(X)$:
$\theta$ generates the data, and $T$ is a deterministic function of the data.
The data-processing inequality then says $I(\theta;T) \le I(\theta;X)$ no
matter what $T$ you pick. A summary can never carry more information about
the parameter than the raw sample.
</p>

<p>
Sufficiency is exactly the case where that inequality is tight. Equivalently:
the reverse chain $\theta \to T \to X$ also holds, because $X \perp \theta
\mid T$. Both chains running at once force $I(\theta;T) = I(\theta;X)$.
$I$ is the strongest currency, since Fisher information is its local
($\Delta\theta \to 0$) limit and the likelihood and variance statements both
follow.
</p>

<p>
Factorization, Rao–Blackwell, and the data-processing inequality are three
readings of that one tightness condition. The sparklines on each edge of the
diagram tell you which currency you're spending.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 7 · X → T(X) → inference, in three flavors" canvasId="fig-flow" width="760" height="440" readoutId="flow-readout">
<Fragment slot="legend">
